\chapter{Lines, Colors and Spectra}\label{ch:analysis}

The analysis part of LineScope is a pipeline of three steps. The selection line is sampled in the
pixel grid of one image; each sample is converted to the chosen color model and reduced to one
channel; the resulting profile goes through a real FFT. This chapter follows that order: first the
line and its sampler, then the color models, and last the spectrum.

\section{The selection line}

The line is shared by all the copies of an image, and the copies differ in size. So the line can't
be stored in pixels. It is stored in \emph{normalized} coordinates instead, where $(0,0)$ is the
top-left corner of the image and $(1,1)$ the bottom-right corner, whatever the size. A resampled
copy only scales the image, so the same normalized line falls on the same content in every copy.
Converting to the pixels of an image of size $W\times H$ is one multiplication per axis:
$x = uW$ and $y = vH$.

