@ The radix-2 FFT of vDSP needs a power-of-two length $N=2^{\lceil\log_2 n\rceil}$. A profile
with fewer than two samples has no spectrum.

<<Spectrum: choose the FFT size>>=
let n = values.count
guard n >= 2 else { return .empty }
let log2n = vDSP_Length(Int(ceil(log2(Double(n)))))
let fftSize = 1 << Int(log2n)
let half = fftSize / 2
@ Removing the mean is optional. Without it, bin $0$ holds the average brightness, which in dB
scale flattens everything else. The window is applied after the mean is removed, so it tapers the
fluctuations and not the offset. Its sum $\sum w_i$ is kept for normalization: with no window it
is simply $n$.

<<Spectrum: condition the signal>>=
var signal = values
if removeMean {
    let mean = vDSP.mean(signal)
    signal = vDSP.add(-mean, signal)
}
var windowSum = Float(n)
if window == .hann {
    // Symmetric Hann over the n real samples.
    var w = [Float](repeating: 0, count: n)
    for i in 0..<n {
        w[i] = 0.5 - 0.5 * cos(2 * Float.pi * Float(i) / Float(n - 1))
    }
    signal = vDSP.multiply(signal, w)
    windowSum = vDSP.sum(w)
}
@ The $n$ conditioned samples are followed by $N-n$ zeros. Zero padding adds no information. It
interpolates the spectrum more finely, so that peaks are drawn at about the right place.

<<Spectrum: zero-pad>>=
var padded = [Float](repeating: 0, count: fftSize)
padded.replaceSubrange(0..<n, with: signal)
@ vDSP computes a real FFT of length $N$ as a complex FFT of length $N/2$. First
[[vDSP_ctoz]] reads the real array as $N/2$ complex numbers: even samples become real parts
and odd samples imaginary parts, in split form. Then [[vDSP_fft_zrip]] transforms it in place.

The output is \emph{packed}. For $1\le k<N/2$, [[real]][$k$] and [[imag]][$k$] hold
$2\operatorname{Re}X_k$ and $2\operatorname{Im}X_k$. The two purely real bins that have no
partner share the first slot: [[real]][0] holds $2X_0$ (the DC term) and [[imag]][0] holds
$2X_{N/2}$ (the Nyquist term). The factor $2$ is vDSP's scaling convention for the forward real
transform.

<<Spectrum: packed real FFT>>=
var real = [Float](repeating: 0, count: half)
var imag = [Float](repeating: 0, count: half)
guard let setup = vDSP_create_fftsetup(log2n, FFTRadix(kFFTRadix2)) else { return .empty }
defer { vDSP_destroy_fftsetup(setup) }

real.withUnsafeMutableBufferPointer { rp in
    imag.withUnsafeMutableBufferPointer { ip in
        var split = DSPSplitComplex(realp: rp.baseAddress!, imagp: ip.baseAddress!)
        padded.withUnsafeBytes { raw in
            vDSP_ctoz(raw.bindMemory(to: DSPComplex.self).baseAddress!, 2, &split, 1, vDSP_Length(half))
        }
        vDSP_fft_zrip(setup, &split, 1, log2n, FFTDirection(FFT_FORWARD))
    }
}
@ The amplitudes are made \emph{single-sided} and normalized so they can be read as the
amplitude of a sinusoid. A real signal $A\cos(2\pi f i+\varphi)$ puts $|X_k| = \frac{A}{2}\sum w_i$
into bin $k$ and the same into its mirror bin $N-k$, which isn't stored. Therefore
\[
  A_k = \frac{2\,|X_k|}{\sum_i w_i}\quad(0<k<N/2),\qquad
  A_0 = \frac{|X_0|}{\sum_i w_i},\qquad
  A_{N/2} = \frac{|X_{N/2}|}{\sum_i w_i},
\]
since DC and Nyquist have no mirror. Dividing by the window sum rather than by $n$ corrects for
the energy the window removed. With it, a sine of amplitude $1$ reads about $1$ with either
window. The halving in the code undoes vDSP's factor $2$.

<<Spectrum: single-sided amplitudes>>=
// vDSP's packed output is scaled by 2; DC is in real[0] and Nyquist in imag[0].
var amplitudes = [Double](repeating: 0, count: half + 1)
let norm = Double(max(windowSum, 1e-12))
amplitudes[0] = abs(Double(real[0]) / 2) / norm
amplitudes[half] = abs(Double(imag[0]) / 2) / norm
for k in 1..<max(half, 1) {
    let re = Double(real[k]) / 2
    let im = Double(imag[k]) / 2
    amplitudes[k] = 2 * (re * re + im * im).squareRoot() / norm
}
@ Bin $k$ of an $N$-point transform of samples spaced $\delta$ pixels apart has frequency
$k/(N\delta)$ cycles per pixel, which reaches $1/(2\delta)\approx\frac12$ at $k=N/2$. The line
spans $(n-1)\delta$ pixels, so the same bin has $k(n-1)/N$ cycles per line.

<<Spectrum: frequency axes>>=
let lineLength = spacing * Double(n - 1)
var cpp = [Double](repeating: 0, count: half + 1)
var cpl = [Double](repeating: 0, count: half + 1)
for k in 0...half {
    cpp[k] = Double(k) / (Double(fftSize) * spacing)
    cpl[k] = cpp[k] * lineLength
}
return SpectrumResult(
    cyclesPerPixel: cpp, cyclesPerLine: cpl, amplitudes: amplitudes,
    fftSize: fftSize, sampleCount: n
)
@
